1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,590
Day one. You are snowed in at school.

2
00:00:05,349 --> 00:00:07,184
The good news: you're not alone.

3
00:00:09,349 --> 00:00:10,267
RAE: This is your fault.

4
00:00:10,547 --> 00:00:13,896
The bad news: it's your crush. And she is not thrilled.

5
00:00:20,826 --> 00:00:26,906
Friday. Two forty-five. Buses leave at three. You forgot your charger. Of course you did.

6
00:00:28,826 --> 00:00:33,136
She stayed late too. To finish a project. Different kind of problem.

7
00:00:35,826 --> 00:00:39,282
The forecast said, light flurries. The forecast lied.

8
00:00:42,826 --> 00:00:50,399
By four, the power's out. By five, the cell tower's down. By six, you realize everyone thinks you went home.

9
00:00:57,176 --> 00:00:58,392
BO: Okay. I have a plan.

10
00:01:05,476 --> 00:01:07,204
It was not a good plan.

11
00:01:10,826 --> 00:01:15,199
Rule one: there's no way out. Every door is under six feet of snow.

12
00:01:22,826 --> 00:01:27,306
Rule two: one vending machine. Thirty-seven snacks. That's the menu.

13
00:01:34,826 --> 00:01:39,946
Rule three: nobody knows you're here. If you want to be found, you'll have to tell someone.

14
00:01:50,826 --> 00:01:54,623
The first thing that tries to take you out isn't hunger. It's cold.

15
00:01:56,826 --> 00:02:08,282
Survival experts have a rule of threes. Three minutes without air. Three hours without shelter in brutal cold. Three days without water. Three weeks without food.

16
00:02:10,826 --> 00:02:16,778
So: shelter. You build a fort. Gym mats for walls. Encyclopedias for insulation.

17
00:02:22,826 --> 00:02:31,551
Your body gives off about a hundred watts of heat. Roughly an old light bulb. Two people in a small fort? Two light bulbs. It helps.

18
00:02:35,826 --> 00:02:37,533
RAE: Can you... leave your light on?

19
00:02:37,813 --> 00:02:43,061
Turns out she's afraid of the dark. Turns out your headphones make a decent nightlight.

20
00:02:45,676 --> 00:02:46,700
RAE: Why'd it go pink?

21
00:02:46,980 --> 00:02:47,919
BO: ...Low battery.

22
00:02:52,826 --> 00:02:55,749
Day two. You're thirsty. There's snow everywhere.

23
00:02:56,029 --> 00:02:56,541
BO: Don't!

24
00:02:56,821 --> 00:02:58,442
Do not eat the snow.

25
00:03:00,826 --> 00:03:06,501
Your body has to spend its own heat to melt it. You'd trade being thirsty for being colder.

26
00:03:10,826 --> 00:03:14,858
Melt it first. Bottles inside your jacket. Slow, but it works.

27
00:03:18,826 --> 00:03:22,879
Is it comfortable? No. Did she laugh for the first time? Yes. Worth it.

28
00:03:28,826 --> 00:03:32,026
Day three. Math class. But the math is your life.

29
00:03:38,826 --> 00:03:51,114
A teenager needs around two thousand calories a day. Thirty-seven snacks is about nine thousand. For two people? Two days. Maybe three, if nobody eats the gummy worms.

30
00:03:58,826 --> 00:04:02,965
So you ration. Half a granola bar each. Measured. With a ruler.

31
00:04:11,476 --> 00:04:12,201
RAE: Seriously?

32
00:04:12,481 --> 00:04:13,975
BO: This felt like an emergency.

33
00:04:25,826 --> 00:04:31,352
And the cold makes it worse. Every shiver is your body burning fuel to stay warm.

34
00:05:00,826 --> 00:05:10,469
Day four. You find Mister Pickett's emergency shelf. A hand-crank radio. A walkie-talkie. And a note that says: Do not touch. Yes, you.

35
00:05:12,826 --> 00:05:15,280
RADIO: ...roads closed for at least three more days...

36
00:05:15,560 --> 00:05:18,312
The radio works. The news doesn't help.

37
00:05:22,826 --> 00:05:29,568
The radio can charge things. You just need a cable. Like the one you came back for. Which you still don't have.

38
00:05:35,826 --> 00:05:40,328
Then the math breaks. You left fifteen snacks last night. There are twelve.

39
00:05:45,826 --> 00:05:47,938
RAE: Three snacks don't just disappear, Bo.

40
00:05:48,218 --> 00:05:48,922
BO: I didn't—

41
00:05:49,202 --> 00:05:53,405
You didn't take them. But you did take the emergency chocolate. So.

42
00:06:00,826 --> 00:06:04,325
Day five. She moves to the gym. Alone. In the dark.

43
00:06:12,826 --> 00:06:16,261
You tell yourself it's fine. Your headphones disagree.

44
00:06:22,826 --> 00:06:32,661
Here's the scary part about cold. It doesn't just hurt. Even mild hypothermia slows your brain. People make worse choices, and don't notice.

45
00:06:50,826 --> 00:06:56,202
You blink. It's gone. The footprints aren't. They start in the middle of the field. From nowhere.

46
00:07:05,826 --> 00:07:10,178
You run to tell her. She's stopped shivering. That should be good news. It isn't.

47
00:07:20,826 --> 00:07:27,056
When your body gets cold enough, it can stop shivering. Not because it's warm. Because it's running out.

48
00:07:35,826 --> 00:07:41,949
You do what you learned on day one. The fort. Body heat. Warm her chest first, not her hands.

49
00:08:11,476 --> 00:08:12,735
RAE: ...Your plans are terrible.

50
00:08:14,215 --> 00:08:14,855
BO: I know.

51
00:08:30,826 --> 00:08:36,437
Day six. She needs a doctor. Rule three: nobody knows you're here. Unless you tell them.

52
00:08:43,876 --> 00:08:45,092
BO: Okay. I have a plan.

53
00:08:45,372 --> 00:08:46,524
RAE: Your plans are terrible.

54
00:08:46,804 --> 00:08:47,935
BO: Yeah. This one's worse.

55
00:09:27,826 --> 00:09:35,912
A minute of cranking buys you about ten minutes of radio. Charging a walkie-talkie? Way less. You crank for an hour.

56
00:09:48,476 --> 00:09:51,356
BO: Hello? Anyone? Westbrook High. Two students. One's sick.

57
00:10:00,826 --> 00:10:03,663
PLOW: ...County Plow Seven. Say again, kid?

58
00:10:03,944 --> 00:10:08,253
Your mood light is dead. You don't need it. Everyone can see your face.

59
00:10:15,826 --> 00:10:19,133
Day seven. The sun comes out. So does a snowplow.

60
00:10:36,476 --> 00:10:40,721
FIREFIGHTER: We swept the building with thermal. Three heat signatures. Where's the third kid?

61
00:10:47,002 --> 00:10:47,898
...Three.

62
00:11:00,826 --> 00:11:03,813
Two weeks later. Everything's normal. Mostly.

63
00:11:10,826 --> 00:11:15,456
Your headphones work again. Sort of. They only seem to have one color now.

64
00:11:40,826 --> 00:11:48,144
Would you give up the thing that makes you, you, to save someone? And who was the third heat signature? Tell me in the comments.
